 @bigbuckoramma  Inflation doesn't account for how much the price of GPUs has gone up, GPUs are WAY above the inflation curve. Supply and demand + price gouging (all along the chain, including AMD/Nvidia) + inflation (the smallest factor) accounts for the pricing.

When the closest competition is a RTX 3060 and that is $800+ from retailers, the bad deal that is a 6600 starts to look like a reasonable deal, it might even be your only option if you need a GPU and have a $500 budget. Yes it sucks, but GPU price creep isn't new, it started years ago and people kept buying.

To fix the problem, we either need more competition in the GPU space, or consumers need to put their foot down and refuse to by GPUs until the prices come back in line with actual inflation.

 @Denominus  No doubt those things play a role. And it is something many of us warned about with the pricing of the Founders Edition cards years ago. And all the fanboys screaming it was just an exclusivity premium...and then every AIB, because of the tiered pricing of chips from NVidia, started selling at ABOVE the FE price.

But the truth is, no one wants to admit just how badly the dollar is doing. All that "stimulus" money was literally printed out of thin air. Nearly 30% of the USD has been "printed" in the last 3 years. The buying power of a dollar is dwindling significantly, regardless of how much the media and the current administration say "everything is fine." There are literally dozens of cargo container ships sitting in the Pacific just inside international waters, so they don't have to make declaration. Because they don't want to devalue their cargo until the dollar falls more.
So this pricing we are seeing, this isn't just price gouging...this IS the new price. Rewind two years ago, remove the percentage of money we have printed, and it's virtually 1:1. And the slop factor is the gouging and supply and demand. All good are following this trend. Electronics is just the first to show it.

